01:07:53,684 --> 01:07:59,684
The Interrupters is a 2011 documentary film, produced by Kartemquin Films, that tells the story of three violence
interrupters who try to protect their Chicago communities from the violence they once employed.

11
01:14:41,052 --> 01:14:47,052
It examines a year in which Chicago drew national headlines
for violence and murder that plagued the city.

12
01:21:28,421 --> 01:21:34,421
The film features the work of CeaseFire, an initiative of
the Chicago Project for Violence Prevention.

13
01:28:15,789 --> 01:28:21,789
In 2004, Tio Hardiman (ex-Director of CeaseFire Illinois) created
and implemented The Violence Interrupter concept.

14
01:35:03,157 --> 01:35:09,157
Violence interrupters Ameena Matthews, Cobe Williams and Eddie Bocanegra look back on their past
experiences with street violence to try to steer young men and women in the right direction.

15
01:41:50,526 --> 01:41:56,526
Matthews, the daughter of former Chicago gang leader Jeff Fort, comes to the aid of the mother of Derrion Albert, a
Chicago high school student whose death made national headlines when it was captured on videotape.

16
01:48:37,894 --> 01:48:43,894
Produced by Kartemquin Films, The Interrupters is directed by Steve James, director of the documentary
Hoop Dreams, and co-produced by Alex Kotlowitz, author of There Are No Children Here.

17
01:55:25,263 --> 01:55:31,263
The film explores themes of hero, social work, redemption, gang leader, honor.

18
02:02:12,631 --> 02:02:18,631
The tone is emotional.
